# __STOCHAFLOW_PROJECT_SLUG__

This repository is a standard Python distribution containing one Stochaflow
extension and room for multiple experiments. Stochaflow discovers the installed
extension through Python package metadata; it does not scan this directory.

Run commands from this project root so relative `data/` and `outputs/` paths in
experiment configuration resolve predictably.

## What was generated

| Path | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `pyproject.toml` | Declares the installable distribution and its `stochaflow.extensions` entry point. |
| `src/__STOCHAFLOW_PACKAGE_NAME__/stochaflow_ext/__init__.py` | Imports the registration modules; keep it free of data loading and task execution. |
| `src/__STOCHAFLOW_PACKAGE_NAME__/stochaflow_ext/data.py` | Builds the synthetic datasets and epoch-aware training DataLoader. |
| `src/__STOCHAFLOW_PACKAGE_NAME__/stochaflow_ext/model.py` | Defines and registers the example model. |
| `src/__STOCHAFLOW_PACKAGE_NAME__/stochaflow_ext/diagnostics.py` | Registers a task-specific RMSE diagnostic. |
| `src/__STOCHAFLOW_PACKAGE_NAME__/stochaflow_ext/training.py` | Composes the model and objective and defines one training step. |
| `src/__STOCHAFLOW_PACKAGE_NAME__/stochaflow_ext/sampling.py` | Produces predictions with a direct-transform SamplingBuilder. |
| `experiments/example/train.yaml` | Selects the installed plugin and configures the example run. |
| `tests/test_extensions.py` | Checks registration and deterministic epoch shuffle behavior. |
| `data/` | Reserved for project-owned data; the generated synthetic example does not read it. |

The example reuses Stochaflow's MSE objective, native PyTorch StepLR scheduler,
EMA lifecycle, TensorBoard logger, and tensor artifact writer. Its custom data,
training, diagnostic, and sampling components remain ordinary Python code
instead of a YAML object graph.

## Install, test, and train with pip

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -e ".[test]"
python -m pytest
stochaflow train --config experiments/example/train.yaml
```

On Windows PowerShell, activate the environment with
`.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1`.

The equivalent `uv` workflow is:

```bash
uv sync --extra test
uv run pytest
uv run stochaflow train --config experiments/example/train.yaml
```

The generated configuration trains a tiny synthetic regression model for two
epochs. A run is written below `outputs/example/`:

```text
outputs/example/<run-id>/
  checkpoints/
    best.pt
    latest.pt
  metrics.jsonl
  resolved_config.yaml
  run_manifest.yaml
  tensorboard/
    __STOCHAFLOW_PROJECT_SLUG__-example/
      events.out.tfevents.*
  samples/final/samples.pt
```

Inspect the local and diagnostic metrics in TensorBoard:

```bash
tensorboard --logdir outputs/example/<run-id>/tensorboard
```

## Strict resume

Resume from a run directory so Stochaflow can validate and inherit both
`latest.pt` and `best.pt`. The checkpoint config and complete training state are
authoritative; do not pass `--config` to a strict resume. `--epochs` is the new
total target, not a number of extra epochs:

```bash
stochaflow train \
  --resume outputs/example/<run-id> \
  --epochs 4
```

Resume creates a new sibling run directory and records its checkpoint lineage.
The generated epoch-aware sampler rebuilds each shuffled training order from the
experiment seed and absolute epoch, so an epoch resumed in a new process uses
the same index order as an uninterrupted run.

With `uv`, prefix the command with `uv run`.

## Sample from a checkpoint

Run the registered direct-transform SamplingBuilder with the trained model:

```bash
stochaflow sample \
  --checkpoint outputs/example/<run-id>/checkpoints/best.pt \
  --output-dir outputs/checkpoint-sample
```

The command writes `samples.pt` and `resolved_sampling.yaml` to the selected
output directory. With no external sampling config, the checkpoint's saved
configuration is the base; sampling CLI flags may still override supported
runtime values.
